Why Repair Your Sofa Instead of Replacing It?
A good sofa is built on a solid wooden frame, and that frame often outlasts the foam and fabric on top of it. Many sofas that look worn out are structurally fine. The seat has flattened, a spring has snapped or a seam has opened, and all of that can be fixed.
Here is why repair makes sense:
•	It costs far less. A repair is usually a fraction of the price of a comparable new sofa. Quality sofas with solid wood frames are expensive to replace.
•	You keep a sofa you already like. The size, shape and style you chose stay the same. Many new sofas use weaker frames and thinner foam than older ones.
•	It is faster. New sofas can take weeks to arrive. Most repairs are finished in a day or two.
•	It creates less waste. Repairing keeps a bulky piece of furniture out of the landfill.
•	You can upgrade while repairing. Replace tired foam with higher-density foam and the sofa can feel better than when it was new.

Our Sofa Repair Services
We handle the full range of sofa problems. Here is what we fix and how.
1. Sofa Frame and Joint Repair
The wooden frame is the skeleton of your sofa. Over time, joints loosen, corners crack and legs wobble, especially in sofas that are heavily used, moved often or have suffered termite or moisture damage.
Signs your frame needs repair:
•	Creaking or cracking sounds when you sit
•	The sofa wobbles or tilts to one side
•	Visible cracks in the wooden rails, arms or legs
•	Arms that feel loose or move when you lean on them
What we do: We open the affected area, remove damaged sections, reinforce joints with new wood, glue and screws or brackets, and rebuild weak areas so the frame is solid again. Minor frame repairs are done at your home. For major structural work, we tell you in advance if workshop repair is needed.
2. Foam Replacement and Seat Repair
Flat, sunken or lumpy seats are the most common sofa complaint. Foam breaks down with years of use, and no amount of fluffing brings it back.
Signs you need new foam:
•	Seats sink in the middle or have a permanent dip
•	The sofa feels hard because the foam has compressed
•	Back cushions slump forward and no longer support you
•	You sink in and find it difficult to stand up
What we do: We remove the old foam and fit new foam cut to the exact size of your seat or back. You can choose the firmness that suits you, from soft and plush to firm and supportive. We also add a layer of wrapping fibre or dacron so the cushion looks full and the cover sits smoothly.
3. Sofa Spring Repair
A broken or sagging spring causes uneven seating and sometimes pokes through the fabric. Springs can be re-tied, replaced or re-tensioned.
Signs of spring problems:
•	A hard spot or lump in the seat
•	A twanging or squeaking sound
•	One side sits lower than the other
•	A spring pressing through the cover
What we do: We replace broken springs, re-tie loose ones and renew the webbing or support base so the seat has even, lasting support.
4. Sofa Cushion Repair
Loose, lumpy or flat cushions can be restored without buying a new sofa. We refill cushions, replace foam inserts, repair zips and rebuild shape so they look and feel right again. We also make new cushion covers if the old ones are beyond saving. Matching fabric can be chosen from our range.
5. Stitching, Seam and Fabric Repair
Split seams, torn fabric, pet scratches and worn armrests make a sofa look much older than it is.
What we do: We re-stitch opened seams, patch or replace damaged panels, repair piping and trim, and match fabric as closely as possible so the repair blends in. If the whole sofa is worn, we can recommend full reupholstery instead.
6. Sofa Polish and Wooden Finish Touch-Up
Faded, scratched or dull wooden arms, legs and exposed frames can be sanded, polished and re-finished. This gives wooden parts a fresh, even look and protects the wood from further damage. For wooden sofas, see our Wooden Sofa Repair page.
7. Recliner, Sofa Chair and Sofa Cum Bed Repair
Besides regular sofas, we also repair sofa chairs and loungers, and fix mechanisms on sofa cum beds, including hinges, locks, folding frames and wheels. See Sofa Cum Bed Repair and Sofa Chair Repair.
8. Leather and Leatherette Repair
Cracks, peeling, tears and fading on leather or leatherette sofas can be repaired with stitching, patching, foam correction and colour touch-up. Details on our Leather Sofa Repair page.

